From 65c9ba67dcc6588526b86775b7c331235949d319 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 00:30:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 174/175] fix: a moving camera dragged the picture over a
 static speckle layer - DSNU is a residual

The digital fixed-pattern terms are hashed from the photosite index and carry no seed and no
frame index, which is right: a sensor's dark-signal non-uniformity is a property of the silicon,
not of the moment. The consequence is that it does not move with the picture, so panning on CCD
or Phone slid the scene across a stationary field of speckles - visible as a layer of grit stuck
to the screen underneath the moving noise.

That is what a RAW, UNCALIBRATED sensor does, and it is not what any delivered footage looks
like, because every real body removes it before the frame leaves: a dark frame is subtracted, or
the optical-black rows set the black level per frame. Only a residual survives - the part the
calibration frame's own noise cannot resolve. The dsnu column was carrying the raw datasheet
figure; it now carries that residual.

  CMOS   1.0  -> 0.15 e-   (15 % residual after per-frame optical-black correction)
  CCD    3.0  -> 0.45 e-   (15 %, same correction from a larger raw DSNU)
  Phone  1.0  -> 0.06 e-   (6 %: a per-unit factory calibration table on top of the per-frame
                            black level, and burst alignment smears what is left)

In the r_FilmGrainDebug 3 per-term table, green channel, four stops under mid grey at ISO 12800
(sigma_cv = 255 * kDsnu * k * attenuation * dsRGB/dlinear, k = 1/(fullWell*isoBase/iso),
attenuation = 1/sqrt(nPix^E * stackN) with E = 0):

  CMOS    0.410 -> 0.061 CV
  CCD     7.373 -> 1.106 CV
  Phone  26.756 -> 1.605 CV

All inside the brief of 1 CV for CMOS and 2 CV for CCD / Phone. PRNU needed no change and was
checked at the same point: at mid grey, ISO 12800, it is 0.274 / 0.439 / 0.224 CV, already under
half a code value, because PRNU is a gain error and so scales with the signal instead of sitting
in the shadows the way an offset does.

The shader, the renderer and every other preset column are untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
